std.strings — Text operations on string values
================================================

The `std.strings` module provides byte-oriented operations on
`string` values: searching, trimming, case conversion,
replacement, and simple transformations. It is the text-layer
counterpart to `std.bytes` (which provides the same API surface
over `[]byte`).

All operations treat strings as sequences of bytes, not runes.
`contains`, `index`, `count` etc. find byte substrings. Case
conversion (`to_upper`, `to_lower`) and whitespace detection
(`trim_space`) handle ASCII only; Unicode-aware variants will
live in a future `std.unicode` module.

Every function that produces a `string` allocates a fresh
buffer. Sysl strings are immutable; there is no zero-copy
substring view today. Operations that don't change the string
(like `contains`) don't allocate.

Dependencies: `std.builder` (for assembling result strings).


    module std.strings

    import std.builder.*


Searching
---------

### has_prefix

`has_prefix(s, prefix)` returns `true` iff `s` begins with
`prefix`. The empty string is a prefix of every string.

    has_prefix(s: string, prefix: string) -> bool
        val n = len(s)
        val m = len(prefix)

        if m > n then return false

        for i in 0..<m
            if s[i] != prefix[i] then return false

        true


### has_suffix

`has_suffix(s, suffix)` returns `true` iff `s` ends with
`suffix`.

    has_suffix(s: string, suffix: string) -> bool
        val n = len(s)
        val m = len(suffix)

        if m > n then return false

        for i in 0..<m
            if s[n - m + i] != suffix[i] then return false

        true


### index

`index(s, sub)` returns the byte offset of the first
occurrence of `sub` in `s`, or `-1` if not found. The empty
substring is found at position 0.

This is a naive O(n*m) search. A Boyer-Moore or similar
sub-linear search may replace it later; the observable
behavior is unchanged.

    index(s: string, sub: string) -> int
        val n = len(s)
        val m = len(sub)

        if m == 0 then return 0
        if m > n then return -1

        val last = n - m

        for i in 0..last
            var matched = true

            for j in 0..<m
                if s[i + j] != sub[j]
                    matched = false
                    break

            if matched then return i

        -1


### last_index

`last_index(s, sub)` returns the byte offset of the last
occurrence of `sub` in `s`, or `-1` if not found. The empty
substring matches at position `len(s)`.

    last_index(s: string, sub: string) -> int
        val n = len(s)
        val m = len(sub)

        if m == 0 then return n
        if m > n then return -1

        var i = n - m

        while i >= 0
            var matched = true

            for j in 0..<m
                if s[i + j] != sub[j]
                    matched = false
                    break

            if matched then return i

            i--

        -1


### contains

`contains(s, sub)` returns `true` iff `sub` appears in `s`.

    contains(s: string, sub: string) -> bool = index(s, sub) >= 0


### count

`count(s, sub)` returns the number of non-overlapping
occurrences of `sub` in `s`. When `sub` is empty, returns
`len(s) + 1` (matching Go's behavior: one match at each
byte boundary, including the ends).

    count(s: string, sub: string) -> int
        val m = len(sub)

        if m == 0 then return len(s) + 1

        var i = 0
        var n = 0
        val last = len(s) - m

        while i <= last
            var matched = true

            for j in 0..<m
                if s[i + j] != sub[j]
                    matched = false
                    break

            if matched
                n++
                i += m
            else
                i++

        n


### index_byte / last_index_byte

Byte-level search. Faster than the string-sub variants when
looking for a single character.

    index_byte(s: string, b: byte) -> int
        val n = len(s)

        for i in 0..<n
            if s[i] == b then return i

        -1

    last_index_byte(s: string, b: byte) -> int
        var i = len(s) - 1

        while i >= 0
            if s[i] == b then return i
            i--

        -1


Substring extraction
--------------------

### substring

`substring(s, lo, hi)` returns `s[lo..hi]` as a new string.
Callers must ensure `0 <= lo <= hi <= len(s)`; out-of-range
indices are a bug.

    substring(s: string, lo: int, hi: int) -> string
        var b = new_builder_cap(hi - lo)

        for i in lo..<hi
            b.write_byte(s[i])

        b.to_str()


Trimming
--------

Helper for ASCII whitespace detection — space, tab, newline,
carriage return, vertical tab, form feed.

    is_ascii_space(b: byte) -> bool
        b == byte(' ') || b == byte('\t') || b == byte('\n') || b == byte('\r') || b == 0x0Bu8 || b == 0x0Cu8


### trim_space

`trim_space(s)` removes leading and trailing ASCII whitespace.

    trim_space(s: string) -> string
        val n = len(s)
        var lo = 0
        var hi = n

        while lo < hi && is_ascii_space(s[lo]) do lo++
        while hi > lo && is_ascii_space(s[hi - 1]) do hi--

        substring(s, lo, hi)


Helper: is the byte `b` present anywhere in `cutset`.

    in_cutset(b: byte, cutset: string) -> bool
        val n = len(cutset)

        for i in 0..<n
            if cutset[i] == b then return true

        false


### trim_left / trim_right / trim

`trim_left(s, cutset)` removes leading bytes that appear in
`cutset`. `trim_right` trims from the end. `trim` trims both.

    trim_left(s: string, cutset: string) -> string
        val n = len(s)
        var lo = 0

        while lo < n && in_cutset(s[lo], cutset) do lo++

        substring(s, lo, n)

    trim_right(s: string, cutset: string) -> string
        val n = len(s)
        var hi = n

        while hi > 0 && in_cutset(s[hi - 1], cutset) do hi--

        substring(s, 0, hi)

    trim(s: string, cutset: string) -> string
        val n = len(s)
        var lo = 0
        var hi = n

        while lo < hi && in_cutset(s[lo], cutset) do lo++
        while hi > lo && in_cutset(s[hi - 1], cutset) do hi--

        substring(s, lo, hi)


Case conversion (ASCII)
-----------------------

`to_upper(s)` maps ASCII lowercase letters to uppercase and
leaves everything else untouched. `to_lower(s)` is symmetric.
Non-ASCII bytes pass through unchanged.

    to_upper(s: string) -> string
        val n = len(s)
        var b = new_builder_cap(n)

        for i in 0..<n
            val c = s[i]

            if c >= byte('a') && c <= byte('z') then b.write_byte(c - 0x20u8)
            else b.write_byte(c)

        b.to_str()

    to_lower(s: string) -> string
        val n = len(s)
        var b = new_builder_cap(n)

        for i in 0..<n
            val c = s[i]

            if c >= byte('A') && c <= byte('Z') then b.write_byte(c + 0x20u8)
            else b.write_byte(c)

        b.to_str()


Replacement
-----------

### replace_all

`replace_all(s, old, repl)` returns a copy of `s` with every
non-overlapping occurrence of `old` replaced by `new`. When
`old` is empty, `new` is inserted between every byte of `s`
and at both ends, matching Go's behavior.

    replace_all(s: string, old: string, repl: string) -> string
        val n = len(s)
        val m = len(old)
        var b = new_builder_cap(n)

        if m == 0

Empty pattern: per Go's semantics, insert `repl` at the start, after
every byte, and at the end — yielding `n + 1` insertions.

            b.write(repl)

            for i in 0..<n
                b.write_byte(s[i])
                b.write(repl)

            return b.to_str()

        var i = 0
        val last = n - m

        while i <= last
            var matched = true

            for j in 0..<m
                if s[i + j] != old[j]
                    matched = false
                    break

            if matched
                b.write(repl)
                i += m
            else
                b.write_byte(s[i])
                i++

The main loop only iterates while a match could still start (`i <=
n - m`); the trailing $m - 1$ bytes can't begin a pattern of length
$m$, so flush them straight into the output.

        while i < n
            b.write_byte(s[i])
            i++

        b.to_str()


### repeat

`repeat(s, n)` returns `s` concatenated with itself `n`
times. `repeat(s, 0)` returns `""`.

    repeat(s: string, n: int) -> string
        if n <= 0 then return ""

        val slen = len(s)
        var b = new_builder_cap(slen * n)

        for i in 0..<n
            b.write(s)

        b.to_str()


Split and join
--------------

### split

`split(s, sep)` splits `s` by `sep` and returns a slice of
the parts. If `sep` is empty, splits into individual bytes
as single-character strings. Adjacent separators produce
empty strings in the result.

    split(s: string, sep: string) -> []string
        val m = len(sep)

        if m == 0

Empty separator splits the string into individual single-byte
strings — matches Go's `strings.Split` (and is the natural inverse
of `join` with `sep == ""`).

            val n = len(s)
            val parts = new [n]string

            for i in 0..<n
                parts[i] = substring(s, i, i + 1)

            return parts[:]

        val n = count(s, sep) + 1
        val parts = new [n]string
        var start = 0
        var pi = 0
        val slen = len(s)
        val last = slen - m

        var i = 0

        while i <= last
            var matched = true

            for j in 0..<m
                if s[i + j] != sep[j]
                    matched = false
                    break

            if matched
                parts[pi] = substring(s, start, i)
                pi++
                i += m
                start = i
            else
                i++

        parts[pi] = substring(s, start, slen)
        parts[:]


### join

`join(parts, sep)` concatenates the strings in `parts` with
`sep` between each pair. `join` of an empty slice returns `""`.

    join(parts: []string, sep: string) -> string
        val n = len(parts)

        if n == 0 then return ""
        if n == 1 then return parts[0]

        var b = new_builder()

        for i in 0..<n
            if i > 0 then b.write(sep)

            b.write(parts[i])

        b.to_str()


### fields

`fields(s)` splits `s` on runs of ASCII whitespace. Leading
and trailing whitespace is ignored. Multiple consecutive
whitespace characters are treated as a single separator.

    fields(s: string) -> []string
        val slen = len(s)

Two-pass: first count the number of fields so we can allocate the
result slice once with the exact size, then walk again to populate
it. Single-pass `append` would allocate logarithmically; the
prepass avoids the wasted intermediate buffers.

        var nf = 0
        var in_word = false
        var i = 0

        while i < slen
            if is_ascii_space(s[i])
                in_word = false
            else
                if !in_word
                    nf++
                    in_word = true
            i++

        val parts = new [nf]string
        var pi = 0
        var start = 0
        in_word = false
        i = 0

        while i < slen
            if is_ascii_space(s[i])
                if in_word
                    parts[pi] = substring(s, start, i)
                    pi++
                    in_word = false
            else
                if !in_word
                    start = i
                    in_word = true
            i++

        if in_word
            parts[pi] = substring(s, start, slen)

        parts[:]


Tests
=====

### has_prefix / has_suffix

    #test
    test_has_prefix_empty() -> unit
        assert(has_prefix("hello", ""), "empty prefix always matches")

    #test
    test_has_prefix_full() -> unit
        assert(has_prefix("hello", "hello"), "full match")

    #test
    test_has_prefix_partial() -> unit
        assert(has_prefix("hello world", "hello"), "partial")

    #test
    test_has_prefix_no_match() -> unit
        assert(!has_prefix("hello", "world"), "no match")

    #test
    test_has_prefix_too_long() -> unit
        assert(!has_prefix("hi", "hello"), "prefix longer than string")

    #test
    test_has_suffix_empty() -> unit
        assert(has_suffix("hello", ""), "empty suffix always matches")

    #test
    test_has_suffix_full() -> unit
        assert(has_suffix("hello", "hello"), "full match")

    #test
    test_has_suffix_partial() -> unit
        assert(has_suffix("hello world", "world"), "trailing")

    #test
    test_has_suffix_no_match() -> unit
        assert(!has_suffix("hello", "world"), "no match")


### index / last_index / contains

    #test
    test_index_empty_sub() -> unit
        assert(index("abc", "") == 0, "empty at 0")

    #test
    test_index_found_at_start() -> unit
        assert(index("hello world", "hello") == 0, "at start")

    #test
    test_index_found_middle() -> unit
        assert(index("hello world", "lo w") == 3, "middle")

    #test
    test_index_found_end() -> unit
        assert(index("hello world", "world") == 6, "at end")

    #test
    test_index_not_found() -> unit
        assert(index("hello", "xyz") == -1, "not found")

    #test
    test_index_sub_longer() -> unit
        assert(index("hi", "hello") == -1, "sub longer than s")

    #test
    test_last_index_empty_sub() -> unit
        assert(last_index("abc", "") == 3, "empty at len")

    #test
    test_last_index_repeated() -> unit
        assert(last_index("banana", "an") == 3, "last 'an' in banana")

    #test
    test_last_index_not_found() -> unit
        assert(last_index("banana", "xyz") == -1, "not found")

    #test
    test_contains_yes() -> unit
        assert(contains("hello world", "world"), "found")

    #test
    test_contains_no() -> unit
        assert(!contains("hello", "xyz"), "not found")


### count

    #test
    test_count_basic() -> unit
        assert(count("banana", "an") == 2, "non-overlapping")

    #test
    test_count_all_overlap_avoided() -> unit
        assert(count("aaaa", "aa") == 2, "non-overlapping counts 2, not 3")

    #test
    test_count_not_found() -> unit
        assert(count("banana", "xyz") == 0, "zero")

    #test
    test_count_empty_sub() -> unit
        assert(count("abc", "") == 4, "len(s) + 1")


### index_byte

    #test
    test_index_byte_found() -> unit
        assert(index_byte("hello", byte('l')) == 2, "first 'l'")

    #test
    test_index_byte_not_found() -> unit
        assert(index_byte("hello", byte('z')) == -1, "not found")

    #test
    test_last_index_byte_found() -> unit
        assert(last_index_byte("hello", byte('l')) == 3, "last 'l'")


### substring

    #test
    test_substring_basic() -> unit
        assert(substring("hello world", 6, 11) == "world", "tail")

    #test
    test_substring_empty_range() -> unit
        assert(substring("hello", 2, 2) == "", "empty")

    #test
    test_substring_full() -> unit
        assert(substring("hello", 0, 5) == "hello", "full")


### trim variants

    #test
    test_trim_space_both_sides() -> unit
        assert(trim_space("  hello  ") == "hello", "both sides")

    #test
    test_trim_space_tabs_and_newlines() -> unit
        assert(trim_space("\t\nhi\r\n") == "hi", "mixed whitespace")

    #test
    test_trim_space_no_whitespace() -> unit
        assert(trim_space("hello") == "hello", "untouched")

    #test
    test_trim_space_all_whitespace() -> unit
        assert(trim_space("   ") == "", "becomes empty")

    #test
    test_trim_left_cutset() -> unit
        assert(trim_left("xxhelloxx", "x") == "helloxx", "left only")

    #test
    test_trim_right_cutset() -> unit
        assert(trim_right("xxhelloxx", "x") == "xxhello", "right only")

    #test
    test_trim_cutset_both() -> unit
        assert(trim("xxhelloxx", "x") == "hello", "both")

    #test
    test_trim_multi_char_cutset() -> unit
        assert(trim("!?hello?!", "!?") == "hello", "any of the chars")


### case conversion

    #test
    test_to_upper_basic() -> unit
        assert(to_upper("hello") == "HELLO", "letters")

    #test
    test_to_upper_mixed() -> unit
        assert(to_upper("Hello, World!") == "HELLO, WORLD!", "mixed")

    #test
    test_to_upper_already_upper() -> unit
        assert(to_upper("ABC") == "ABC", "idempotent")

    #test
    test_to_lower_basic() -> unit
        assert(to_lower("HELLO") == "hello", "letters")

    #test
    test_to_lower_mixed() -> unit
        assert(to_lower("Hello, World!") == "hello, world!", "mixed")

### replace_all

    #test
    test_replace_all_basic() -> unit
        assert(replace_all("hello world", "world", "there") == "hello there", "one")

    #test
    test_replace_all_multiple() -> unit
        assert(replace_all("aaa", "a", "b") == "bbb", "each char")

    #test
    test_replace_all_no_match() -> unit
        assert(replace_all("hello", "xyz", "abc") == "hello", "untouched")

    #test
    test_replace_all_longer_replacement() -> unit
        assert(replace_all("hi hi", "hi", "hello") == "hello hello", "grows")

    #test
    test_replace_all_shorter_replacement() -> unit
        assert(replace_all("hello hello", "hello", "hi") == "hi hi", "shrinks")

    #test
    test_replace_all_non_overlapping() -> unit
        assert(replace_all("aaaa", "aa", "X") == "XX", "non-overlap")


### repeat

    #test
    test_repeat_basic() -> unit
        assert(repeat("ab", 3) == "ababab", "three times")

    #test
    test_repeat_zero() -> unit
        assert(repeat("abc", 0) == "", "zero")

    #test
    test_repeat_negative() -> unit
        assert(repeat("abc", -5) == "", "negative treated as zero")

    #test
    test_repeat_empty_string() -> unit
        assert(repeat("", 100) == "", "empty stays empty")

    #test
    test_repeat_one() -> unit
        assert(repeat("abc", 1) == "abc", "identity")


### split

    #test
    test_split_basic() -> unit
        val parts = split("a,b,c", ",")
        assert(len(parts) == 3, "three parts")
        assert(parts[0] == "a", "first")
        assert(parts[1] == "b", "second")
        assert(parts[2] == "c", "third")

    #test
    test_split_no_match() -> unit
        val parts = split("hello", ",")
        assert(len(parts) == 1, "one part")
        assert(parts[0] == "hello", "whole string")

    #test
    test_split_empty_sep() -> unit
        val parts = split("abc", "")
        assert(len(parts) == 3, "three chars")
        assert(parts[0] == "a", "a")
        assert(parts[1] == "b", "b")
        assert(parts[2] == "c", "c")

    #test
    test_split_adjacent_separators() -> unit
        val parts = split("a,,b", ",")
        assert(len(parts) == 3, "three parts")
        assert(parts[0] == "a", "first")
        assert(parts[1] == "", "empty middle")
        assert(parts[2] == "b", "third")

    #test
    test_split_leading_trailing() -> unit
        val parts = split(",a,b,", ",")
        assert(len(parts) == 4, "four parts")
        assert(parts[0] == "", "leading empty")
        assert(parts[3] == "", "trailing empty")

    #test
    test_split_multi_char_sep() -> unit
        val parts = split("one::two::three", "::")
        assert(len(parts) == 3, "three parts")
        assert(parts[0] == "one", "first")
        assert(parts[1] == "two", "second")
        assert(parts[2] == "three", "third")


### join

    #test
    test_join_basic() -> unit
        val parts = split("a,b,c", ",")
        assert(join(parts, "-") == "a-b-c", "rejoin")

    #test
    test_join_empty_sep() -> unit
        val parts = split("a,b,c", ",")
        assert(join(parts, "") == "abc", "no separator")

    #test
    test_join_single() -> unit
        val parts = new [1]string
        parts[0] = "hello"
        assert(join(parts[:], ",") == "hello", "single")

    #test
    test_join_empty_parts() -> unit
        val parts: []string = []
        assert(join(parts, ",") == "", "empty")


### fields

    #test
    test_fields_basic() -> unit
        val parts = fields("  hello   world  ")
        assert(len(parts) == 2, "two words")
        assert(parts[0] == "hello", "first")
        assert(parts[1] == "world", "second")

    #test
    test_fields_tabs_newlines() -> unit
        val parts = fields("\thello\n\nworld\t")
        assert(len(parts) == 2, "two words")
        assert(parts[0] == "hello", "first")
        assert(parts[1] == "world", "second")

    #test
    test_fields_no_whitespace() -> unit
        val parts = fields("hello")
        assert(len(parts) == 1, "one word")
        assert(parts[0] == "hello", "whole")

    #test
    test_fields_all_whitespace() -> unit
        val parts = fields("   ")
        assert(len(parts) == 0, "no words")

    #test
    test_fields_empty() -> unit
        val parts = fields("")
        assert(len(parts) == 0, "no words")


Design notes
------------

**Byte-oriented.** Strings are byte sequences. `index` finds
byte substrings; `count` counts byte matches; `to_upper`
touches ASCII only. A future `std.unicode` module will layer
rune-aware operations (casefolding, grapheme clusters,
normalization) on top.

**Naive search algorithms.** `index`, `last_index`, `count`,
and `replace_all` use O(n*m) byte-by-byte matching. Boyer-Moore
or Knuth-Morris-Pratt can replace them later without API
changes. For the input sizes typical in systems code (paths,
identifiers, short messages) the naive approach is competitive.

**Every function allocates when it produces a string.** Sysl
strings are immutable and don't share backing arrays, so
`substring`, `trim_space`, etc. copy. If allocation becomes
a bottleneck, a sliced-string type with shared backing can
come later — the API won't change, the representation will.

**`is_ascii_space` only.** Go's `strings.TrimSpace` uses
Unicode's whitespace set. This module intentionally stays
ASCII-only for now — pulling in Unicode category data is
a bigger commitment than a byte-level primitive should make.
